<script setup lang="ts">
import { shallowRef } from "vue";
import { PhArrowLeft, PhBookOpen, PhCopy, PhGearSix, PhRobot } from "@phosphor-icons/vue";
import { extensionAuthoringPrompt } from "@/lib/extensionAuthoring";
defineEmits<{ close: []; onboarding: []; shortcuts: [] }>();
const page = shallowRef<"welcome" | "extensions">("welcome");
const copiedPrompt = shallowRef(false);

async function copyExtensionPrompt() {
  await navigator.clipboard.writeText(extensionAuthoringPrompt);
  copiedPrompt.value = true;
  window.setTimeout(() => { copiedPrompt.value = false; }, 1800);
}
</script>

<template>
  <section class="docs-screen" aria-label="Burrow documentation">
    <header class="docs-header"><button type="button" @click="$emit('close')"><PhArrowLeft :size="16" /> Back</button><span><PhBookOpen :size="16" /> Documentation</span></header>
    <div class="docs-body"><aside><p>GETTING STARTED</p><button :class="{ active: page === 'welcome' }" @click="page = 'welcome'">Welcome to Burrow</button><button @click="$emit('onboarding')">Run onboarding again</button><button @click="$emit('shortcuts')">Keyboard shortcuts</button><p>WORKSPACES</p><button>Projects and threads</button><button>Agents and terminals</button><p>EXTENSIONS</p><button :class="{ active: page === 'extensions' }" @click="page = 'extensions'">Build an extension</button><p>SETTINGS</p><button><PhGearSix :size="14" /> Preferences</button></aside>
      <article v-if="page === 'welcome'"><p class="docs-kicker">GETTING STARTED</p><h1>Welcome to Burrow</h1><p class="lead">Burrow is a native workspace for running and coordinating AI coding agents without losing track of the work around them.</p><h2>Start with a project</h2><p>Open a folder, then use the composer to begin a chat or start an agent in a terminal. Every project keeps its terminals, threads, and Git context together.</p><h2>Keep agent work visible</h2><p>Agent status is surfaced in the workspace, so you can keep coding while Burrow tells you when a result needs review or an agent is waiting.</p><div class="docs-callout"><PhRobot :size="18" /><span>Need a refresher? Re-run the onboarding from Help at any time.</span></div></article>
      <article v-else><p class="docs-kicker">EXTENSIONS · V1</p><h1>Build an extension</h1><p class="lead">Extensions are local folders. Burrow validates their manifest and runs a declared command only when the user explicitly clicks it.</p><h2>Install an extension</h2><p>Open <strong>Settings → Extensions</strong>, then choose an extension folder or a ZIP archive. The selected source must contain <code>extension.json</code>; Burrow validates it before copying it into its extensions folder. Installing the same ID again replaces that extension with the selected package.</p><h2>Host API</h2><p>Use <code>@burrow/sdk</code> for capability-scoped workspace access, macOS Keychain secrets and task progress. v1 still has no direct tab, terminal, chat or arbitrary custom-UI API.</p><h2>Manifest</h2><pre>{
  "apiVersion": 1,
  "id": "hello-burrow",
  "name": "Hello Burrow",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "commands": [{ "id": "greet", "title": "Say hello", "command": "node", "args": ["index.mjs"] }]
}</pre><p><code>id</code> is lowercase and hyphenated. The executable must be on <code>PATH</code>; it is never evaluated by a shell. Commands have a 15-second limit.</p><h2>Right-panel surfaces</h2><p>Add a <code>surfaces</code> entry with <code>kind: "workspace-pulse"</code> to put a native workspace summary in RP. Burrow renders it using its own theme and Git data, not extension-provided UI code.</p><h2>Give this to an AI</h2><p>Copy a constrained authoring brief for an LLM. It contains the supported contract and prevents it from inventing APIs that do not exist.</p><button class="docs-copy" type="button" @click="copyExtensionPrompt"><PhCopy :size="14" />{{ copiedPrompt ? "Copied" : "Copy extension brief" }}</button><h2>Trust and permissions</h2><p>The permissions list is visible to users, but v1 does not yet provide an OS sandbox. Install only extensions you trust. Extension code runs outside Burrow’s process, so a crash cannot take the app down.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>
</template>

